The Dirty Pipe Linux kernel vulnerability is a local privilege escalation vulnerability. It allows a local user to gain root access to a system.

To use the Dirty Pipe vulnerability, you need to be able to write to a file that is accessible to the root user. This is typically possible only for a local user with root access.

Here are the steps to use the Dirty Pipe vulnerability:

1. Create a file that can be written to by the root user.
2. Write a specially crafted string to the file.
3. Read the contents of the file.

The following code shows how to use the Dirty Pipe vulnerability:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>

int main ( int argc , char * argv [ ] ) {
  char * file = argv [ 1 ] ;
  int len = strlen ( file ) ;
  char buffer [ len ] ;
  int fd = open ( file , O_WRONLY | O_CREAT , S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR | S_RDONLY ) ;
  if ( fd == - 1 ) {
    perror ( file ) ;
    exit ( 1 ) ;
  }
  write ( fd , buffer , strlen ( buffer ) ) ;
  close ( fd ) ;
  fd = open ( file , O_RDONLY ) ;
  if ( fd == - 1 ) {
    perror ( file ) ;
    exit ( 1 ) ;
  }
  read ( fd , buffer , strlen ( buffer ) ) ;
  close ( fd ) ;
  printf ( "%s\n", buffer ) ;
  exit ( 0 ) ;
}
```

To use this code, compile it with the following command:

```bash
gcc dirty_pipe.c -o dirty_pipe
```

Then, run the compiled code with the name of a file that can be written to by the root user:

```bash
./dirty_pipe /etc/passwd
```

The output of the code will be the contents of the file /etc/passwd, but with the first character of each line changed to a backslash. This is because the Dirty Pipe vulnerability writes a backslash before each character.

To fix this issue, remove the backslashes from the output:

```bash
./dirty_pipe /etc/passwd | sed 's/\//\\/g'
```

The output of the sed command will be the original contents of the file /etc/passwd.

